# Deployment settings for the Slack demo, in one place.
#
#   cp slack/deploy/deploy.env.example slack/deploy/deploy.env
#   $EDITOR slack/deploy/deploy.env
#   ./slack/deploy/deploy.sh
#
# deploy.sh and new-project.sh read slack/deploy/deploy.env when it exists.
# It is gitignored: it describes YOUR deployment, and the committed file is
# this example.
#
# NO SECRETS BELONG HERE. The three the service needs live in Secret Manager,
# created once by deploy.sh; the only secret-adjacent thing this file may hold
# is a *path* to one (the *_FILE settings at the bottom).
#
# Every line is written `NAME="${NAME:-value}"` so an exported variable still
# wins: `SERVICE=other ./slack/deploy/deploy.sh` overrides the file, the file
# overrides the built-in defaults, and the defaults are what you get with no
# file at all.

# --- where it goes ----------------------------------------------------------
# The project. Leave it unset to use whatever `gcloud config get-value project`
# reports; new-project.sh writes the project it creates into this file for you.
PROJECT_ID="${PROJECT_ID:-}"
REGION="${REGION:-us-central1}"
# Where Cloud Build runs. Defaults to REGION on purpose: the GLOBAL pool is
# shared and can leave a build QUEUED indefinitely with no diagnostic, while
# the same build in a named region starts in seconds. Change it only if your
# REGION has no Cloud Build.
CLOUDBUILD_REGION="${CLOUDBUILD_REGION:-${REGION}}"
SERVICE="${SERVICE:-judgment-pack-slack-demo}"
AR_REPO="${AR_REPO:-judgment-pack}"

# --- the model --------------------------------------------------------------
# Narration and drafting only: every disposition in this demo comes from the
# jpack binary in the container, which holds no key and opens no connection.
# A smaller model costs less and may need more repair rounds in the authoring
# flow — which that flow shows you rather than hiding, because the validator
# is the gatekeeper either way.
GEMINI_MODEL="${GEMINI_MODEL:-gemini-3.6-flash}"

# --- the service's shape ----------------------------------------------------
# One instance, CPU always allocated: the app acks Slack in under three
# seconds and does the real work on background threads, and every flow reads
# a scratch project and (for use case 3) a live gateway process on this
# instance's own disk. Both break if Cloud Run throttles the CPU between
# requests or runs a second instance — and durable session state does not
# change that. Change these only with that in mind (DESIGN.md).
MIN_INSTANCES="${MIN_INSTANCES:-1}"
MAX_INSTANCES="${MAX_INSTANCES:-1}"
CONCURRENCY="${CONCURRENCY:-20}"
CPU="${CPU:-1}"
MEMORY="${MEMORY:-512Mi}"
TIMEOUT="${TIMEOUT:-300}"

# --- where session state lives ----------------------------------------------
# "firestore" makes a user's PROGRESS durable: where they are in the demo and
# what they have finished survive a restart, a redeploy, or this instance
# being replaced. "memory" keeps it all in the process, which is what the dry
# run and any bare `docker run` use.
#
# What Firestore does NOT buy — say it out loud, because it is the tempting
# next inference — is a multi-instance service. Two things every flow needs
# are local to a container by nature: the scratch copy of the project that
# jpack subprocesses read off this disk, and the live signing gateway with
# its keypair and its store. Those are rebuilt per container, and where they
# cannot be rebuilt (a desk's receipts, a decision book) the user is told
# plainly. MIN/MAX_INSTANCES stay at 1 either way (slack/DESIGN.md).
STATE_BACKEND="${STATE_BACKEND:-firestore}"
# The sessions collection. Two more are derived from it and written to:
# <name>-events (Slack event ids, so a retry is recognized across a restart)
# and <name>-limits (the per-user budgets). deploy.sh enables a TTL policy on
# all three; nothing else ever deletes the derived two.
FIRESTORE_COLLECTION="${FIRESTORE_COLLECTION:-slack-demo-sessions}"
# Leave empty for the project's (default) database, which deploy.sh creates in
# Native mode. Set this only when the default database is Datastore mode — its
# mode cannot be changed, so the answer is a NAMED Native database:
#   gcloud firestore databases create --database=slack-demo \
#     --location=us-central1 --type=firestore-native
FIRESTORE_DATABASE="${FIRESTORE_DATABASE:-}"
# How long an idle session lives before it is expired — the value written to
# each document's expires_at, which the Firestore TTL policy deletes on.
SESSION_TTL_SECONDS="${SESSION_TTL_SECONDS:-7200}"

# --- what the container reads ----------------------------------------------
# The baked demo project, the per-session scratch root, and the authority
# label the per-session signing gateway stamps into every receipt.
DEMO_PROJECT="${DEMO_PROJECT:-/opt/judgment-pack/enterprise-demo}"
SESSION_ROOT="${SESSION_ROOT:-/tmp}"
GATEWAY_AUTHORITY="${GATEWAY_AUTHORITY:-gateway:judgment-pack-slack}"

# --- creating the project (new-project.sh only) -----------------------------
# Both optional. BILLING_ACCOUNT is only consulted when more than one open
# account is visible; ORGANIZATION or FOLDER place the new project.
BILLING_ACCOUNT="${BILLING_ACCOUNT:-}"
ORGANIZATION="${ORGANIZATION:-}"
FOLDER="${FOLDER:-}"

# --- secrets from files, for a shell with no terminal -----------------------
# Paths, never values. deploy.sh prompts with echo off when these are unset
# and the secret is not already in Secret Manager.
# SLACK_BOT_TOKEN_FILE="${SLACK_BOT_TOKEN_FILE:-}"
# SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET_FILE="${SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET_FILE:-}"
# GEMINI_API_KEY_FILE="${GEMINI_API_KEY_FILE:-}"

# The private channel the "Talk to a human" modal posts into (e.g. C0123456789).
# Create it, invite the bot, paste the id. Unset, the human buttons do not
# render at all.
TRIAGE_CHANNEL=
